/* Bản dịch kịch bản «Giải mã tác phẩm» — English (en). Claude dịch ngày 30/10/2026 từ data/works.js, kiểm tra bằng i18n/validate.js.
   {chữ hiện|cách đọc}: người xem thấy phần trước, giọng AI đọc phần sau (số, năm, thế kỷ, chữ khó đọc). */
window.ART_I18N = window.ART_I18N || {};
window.ART_I18N.en = {"lang":"en","v":1,"works":{"dong-ho-dam-cuoi-chuot":{"title":"The Mouse Wedding","artist":"Artisans of Đông Hồ village (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design (Đông Hồ printmaking dates from around the 17th century)","medium":"Woodblock print on dó paper coated with điệp (powdered scallop shell), natural pigments","where":"Đông Hồ painting village (Thuận Thành ward, Bắc Ninh); prints held in many museums","place":{"name":"Đông Hồ painting village","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Đông Hồ","Lunar New Year","satire","woodblock print","Vietnamese folk art"],"hook":"At first glance, it's just a cheerful mouse wedding, the kind of picture people put up for Tết, the Lunar New Year. But look closely, and you'll find a bribe being paid in plain sight, in broad daylight.","intro":"The Mouse Wedding, a woodblock print from Đông Hồ village, Bắc Ninh. Each color has its own block, printed on handmade dó paper coated with crushed seashell.","openers":["This is a wedding for the mouse family, so why is the biggest figure in the picture a cat?","There's a tiny detail next to the mouse offering a bird to the cat: two Chinese characters that read Tống lễ.","Try to find the two mice blowing horns. That music can be read in two completely opposite ways."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a painting of mice…","end":"A mouse picture made for New Year laughs, yet it says so much about power and palm-greasing in an entire society. Now you understand why people can spend hours looking at The Mouse Wedding.","img":[{"by":"Đông Hồ artisans · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🐱 Cat = the powerful","q":"Did you notice who's the biggest figure in the picture?","a":"Not the bride, not the groom, but the cat. It takes up the whole top corner, one paw raised as if giving orders. According to the Đông Hồ artisans, the cat stands for those in power, and the mice are the common people.","s":"Not the bride, but the cat. According to the artisans, the cat stands for the powerful, and the mice for ordinary people."},{"tag":"🐦 Tống lễ = giving gifts","q":"What is the lead mouse offering to the cat?","a":"A bird. Right below it are two Chinese characters, Tống lễ, meaning 'delivering gifts'. If the wedding is to go off in peace, the mice have to grease the cat's palm first.","s":"A bird, labeled Tống lễ, 'delivering gifts'. For a peaceful wedding, the cat has to be paid off first."},{"tag":"🐟 Fish and bird","q":"And what's the second mouse holding?","a":"A carp. Birds and fish are a cat's favorite treats. Look at how the gift-bearing mice bow and scrape: that's the posture of someone who's afraid."},{"tag":"🎺 Tác nhạc = strike up","q":"Are those two horn-blowing mice celebrating the wedding, or up to something else?","a":"Nearby are two characters, Tác nhạc, meaning 'strike up the music'. Some say the horns are there to keep the cat happy while it accepts its gifts. Others read it the opposite way: the horns blare so the whole village hears about the bribe."},{"tag":"📜 Four Chinese characters","q":"What do the four Chinese characters at top left say?","a":"The press usually reads them as Lão thử thủ thân, but there's still no agreement on how to read this line or what it means. The mouse wedding is also a theme in Chinese New Year prints, where one version ends with the cat eating the bride. In Đông Hồ, the craftsmen kept the gift-giving scene and turned it into satire."},{"tag":"🐴 The groom on horseback","q":"So where's the groom?","a":"According to artisan Nguyễn Hữu Sam, here he is: riding a rosy-red horse, wearing a hat, dressed like a mandarin, with a parasol held up behind him. Some sources read the characters beside him as Chủ hôn, the person hosting the wedding. Whoever he is, this mouse wedding is staged exactly like a wedding of the elite.","s":"On a rosy-red horse, in a hat, dressed like a mandarin (some read him as the wedding host): the mouse wedding copies a wedding of the elite."},{"tag":"👰 The bridal palanquin","q":"Where's the bride sitting?","a":"In a yellow-roofed palanquin carried by four mice. There are signboards, parasols and music, everything a proper bridal procession needs. And it's that very pomp that makes the bribery scene above all the more ironic.","s":"In a yellow-roofed palanquin carried by four mice, as grand as any proper bridal procession."},{"tag":"〰️ Two-tier storytelling","q":"Why is there a wavy line splitting the picture in two?","a":"Folk paintings don't use Western-style perspective. That wavy line separates two scenes: on top, the procession bringing gifts to the cat; below, the procession escorting the bride. The two stories follow each other like two panels of a comic strip."},{"tag":"❔ Five black dots","q":"And what about the five little black dots on the left?","a":"The honest answer: no one knows for sure. For dots like these on mouse paintings, artisan Nguyễn Hữu Sam reads them as the five elements, a wish for the mouse family to thrive in harmony. That's one reading, not the only answer."},{"tag":"🎨 Colors from nature","q":"Where do these colors come from?","a":"All from nature: red from red-earth pebbles, yellow from pagoda-tree flowers, blue from indigo leaves, black from the ash of bamboo leaves or sticky-rice straw. The shimmering background is powdered scallop shell in sticky-rice paste. Each color has its own block; the black outlines go on last.","s":"Red from red-earth pebbles, yellow from pagoda-tree flowers, blue from indigo, black from bamboo-leaf ash. The shimmer is powdered scallop shell."}]},"dong-ho-lon-nai":{"title":"Herd of Yin-Yang Pigs","artist":"Artisans of Đông Hồ village (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design","medium":"Woodblock print on dó paper coated with điệp (powdered scallop shell), natural pigments","where":"Đông Hồ painting village (Thuận Thành ward, Bắc Ninh)","place":{"name":"Đông Hồ painting village","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Đông Hồ","blessing print","yin and yang","Vietnamese folk art"],"hook":"A big, round mother pig and her piglets, pasted up for Tết, the Lunar New Year. But look at the swirl on her body: it's a whole universe in miniature.","intro":"Herd of Yin-Yang Pigs, a woodblock print from Đông Hồ village. There isn't a single word on it; the picture itself speaks the blessing.","openers":["Why, in many Đông Hồ pig prints, does the mother pig have exactly five piglets, each a different color?","One detail on this pig's body comes straight from how farmers of old picked their breeding stock.","Stop and look at the mother pig's snout: is it in profile, or is it looking straight at you?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a painting of pigs…","end":"Not a single word, just swirls and a herd of plump pigs, yet people long ago packed into it both the philosophy of yin and yang and the dream of plenty. Now you understand why people can look at it for hours.","img":[{"by":"Đông Hồ artisans · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"☯️ Hair whorl = yin-yang","q":"What's that swirl on the mother pig's back?","a":"It's the yin-yang symbol. But it comes from something very down-to-earth: the whorl in a pig's bristles. Farmers of old saw a whorl as the mark of good breeding stock, and the printmakers turned it into a yin-yang circle, a wish for the herd to keep multiplying without end.","s":"A yin-yang symbol, born from a hair whorl, the mark of a good breeder: a wish for endless offspring."},{"tag":"🐷 The Ỉ pig breed","q":"What breed is this pig, to be so round and fat?","a":"It's the Ỉ pig, an old breed from northern Vietnam, with a sagging back and a big belly. Real pigs back then were much skinnier. The picture doesn't show what people had, but what farmers hoped for: a full, well-fed year.","s":"The Ỉ pig, an old northern breed. The picture shows not what people had, but what they wished for: a year of plenty."},{"tag":"👃 Side and front at once","q":"Is this pig's snout shown from the side or from the front?","a":"Both. Writer Phùng Hồng Kổn points out that the snout is drawn in profile and head-on at the same time. Folk artists didn't need the rules of perspective; they drew things the way they'd be seen most clearly."},{"tag":"5️⃣ Five piglets, five hues","q":"How many piglets does the mother have?","a":"In this version, five piglets, each a different color. Artisan Nguyễn Hữu Quả says five is just right for the five elements. That's how the villagers read the picture; the number of piglets can vary from one woodblock to another.","s":"Five, each a different color. Đông Hồ artisans read that as exactly the five elements."},{"tag":"🐖 Kids by the dozen","q":"What's the piglet on the right up to?","a":"Climbing onto its mother, head up as if laughing. Each piglet strikes its own pose: one climbs, one snuggles up to mom, one is busy eating. It's a picture of children and grandchildren by the dozen, the big, lively household people wished for every New Year."},{"tag":"☯️ From mother to piglets","q":"Did you notice the piglets have swirls too?","a":"That's right. The woodblocks have been recarved over many generations, and details may change, but the yin-yang swirl is always kept, like a blessing passed from mother to child."},{"tag":"🌙 Volume from color","q":"What are those crescent shapes on the pig's body for?","a":"That's how the artisans create volume. No cast shadows, no light and shade, just a few curved patches of color, and the pig's body looks round and full."},{"tag":"🌱 Something to eat","q":"And the little plant at the pigs' feet?","a":"Probably food: one piglet is munching on the leaves. For farmers, a herd of well-fed pigs is itself a wish for a prosperous year."},{"tag":"✨ Seashell paper","q":"Why does the background shimmer?","a":"That's the điệp coating: scallop shells ground fine, mixed with sticky-rice paste and brushed onto handmade dó paper. Under the light, the pigs seem to glow. The poet Hoàng Cầm mentioned chicken and pig paintings on shell-coated paper in his poem 'Across the Đuống River'.","s":"The điệp coating: powdered scallop shell and sticky-rice paste brushed onto dó paper, which makes the pigs seem to glow."}]},"dong-ho-ga":{"title":"Đông Hồ Rooster Painting","artist":"Artisans of Đông Hồ village (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design (the custom of hanging rooster paintings dates back to the 16th century)","medium":"Woodblock print on dó paper, natural pigments","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, Hanoi (the rooster-and-hen version shown)","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Đông Hồ","Lunar New Year","five virtues","woodblock print"],"hook":"At Tết, the Lunar New Year, people used to paste rooster paintings on their doors. But that rooster wasn't just decoration: it carries five virtues the ancestors wanted the young to learn.","intro":"A Đông Hồ rooster painting, a woodblock print on handmade dó paper. The version you're seeing has a rooster, a hen and their chicks, and it's now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["That red comb on the rooster's head: whose hat did people of old compare it to?","There's one virtue of the rooster you can't see in this picture, no matter how closely you look.","Try to find the spur on the rooster's leg. To people of old, it stood for an entire virtue."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a painting of a rooster…","end":"Learning, martial skill, courage, benevolence and trust: five virtues wrapped up in one rooster on dó paper. Now you understand why rooster paintings went up right on the front door every spring.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Đông Hồ artisans · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🎓 Comb = Learning","q":"What does the red comb on the rooster's head look like?","a":"People of old compared it to a scholar's hat. So the comb stands for Văn, learning, the first of the rooster's five virtues."},{"tag":"⚔️ Spurs = Martial skill","q":"What virtue are these legs hiding?","a":"A rooster's legs have spurs, weapons made for fighting. The spurs stand for Võ, martial skill."},{"tag":"🦁 Chest out = Courage","q":"And that puffed-out chest?","a":"That's Dũng, courage: meet a rival, charge right in, never back down."},{"tag":"⏰ Crowing on time = Trust","q":"What do the Chinese characters on this rooster painting say?","a":"Dạ xướng ngũ canh hòa: crowing through the night, steady through all five night watches. Back before clocks, the rooster's crow marked each watch. Crowing right on time: that's Tín, trustworthiness."},{"tag":"❤️ Sharing = Benevolence","q":"And the fifth virtue, the one you can't actually see?","a":"It's Nhân, benevolence: when the rooster finds food, he calls the whole flock over to share it. A picture can't show that call, but it can show the result: the hen and her chicks gathered close together."},{"tag":"🐣 The chicks","q":"Can you spot the chick climbing onto its mother's back?","a":"Each chick has its own pose: one climbs on mom's back, one grabs for food, one flaps its wings. A hen with her brood is a wish for a warm home full of children and grandchildren."},{"tag":"🦵 Golden rooster stance","q":"Why is this rooster standing on just one leg?","a":"That's the kim kê độc lập pose, 'the golden rooster stands on one leg': the steady, proud stance of a rooster as it lets out its crow."},{"tag":"🖨️ One block per color","q":"How was this dazzling tail printed?","a":"Each color has its own woodblock, printed one over another; the black outline block goes last, tying all the patches of color together."},{"tag":"🚪 Guarding the door","q":"How long have Vietnamese people hung rooster paintings at Tết?","a":"Since around the sixteenth or seventeenth century: a poem by Hoàng Sĩ Khải describes rooster paintings hung in the capital, Thăng Long, today's Hanoi, at Tết to ward off evil. The rooster's crow calls up the sun and chases away the dark, just what people want for a new year.","s":"Since around the sixteenth or seventeenth century: the rooster calls up the sun and chases away the dark, just what people want for a new year."}]},"hang-trong-ngu-ho":{"title":"The Five Tigers","artist":"Artisans of Hàng Trống street, Hanoi (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design (flourished in the late 19th – early 20th century)","medium":"Half printed, half painted: outlines printed from a woodblock, colors applied by hand on dó paper","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, Hanoi (the version shown)","place":{"name":"Hàng Trống street, Hoàn Kiếm","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Hàng Trống","devotional painting","five elements","Vietnamese folk art"],"hook":"Five tigers, five colors, among clouds and rocks. But follow them clockwise, and the painting will read you the whole cycle of heaven and earth.","intro":"The Five Tigers, a Hàng Trống painting from Hanoi. Only the outlines are printed from a woodblock; the colors are painted by hand, layer by layer, so no two copies are exactly alike.","openers":["Why, in Mother Goddess temples, do the five Lord Tigers sit at the lowest altar and receive offerings of raw meat?","There's a small detail under the yellow tiger's paws: a command tablet, and the writing on it isn't the same in every version.","Stop and look at both sides of the pack: a sword on one side, a command flag on the other, like an army."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours in front of a painting of five tigers…","end":"Five tigers, five colors, five directions, arranged in an endless cycle of creation. Now you understand why people can stand in front of The Five Tigers for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot · Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🟡 Yellow tiger = Earth","q":"Why is the yellow tiger in the middle so much bigger?","a":"That's Hoàng hổ, the Yellow Tiger, linked to Earth, at the center where the other four elements meet. In temples, the yellow tiger commands the whole pack."},{"tag":"⚪ White tiger = Metal","q":"Who's the white tiger riding the clouds up in the corner?","a":"Bạch hổ, the White Tiger: Metal, the west, autumn. The two tigers riding clouds at the top belong to heaven; the two standing on rocks below belong to earth."},{"tag":"⚫ Black tiger = Water","q":"And the black tiger on the right?","a":"Hắc hổ, the Black Tiger: Water, the north, winter."},{"tag":"🔴 Red tiger = Fire","q":"Who's the red tiger snarling in the bottom corner?","a":"Xích hổ, the Red Tiger: Fire, the south, summer. By convention, the remaining one on the right is Thanh hổ, the Green Tiger: Wood, the east, spring. Because the colors are painted by hand, every copy has its own shades."},{"tag":"🔄 The generating cycle","q":"Now go clockwise, starting from the green tiger. What do you see?","a":"In this common color arrangement: green, then red, then yellow in the middle, then white, then black. That's the generating cycle of the five elements: Wood gives rise to Fire, Fire to Earth, Earth to Metal, Metal to Water, and Water back to Wood. The five tigers form a cycle that never ends.","s":"Green, red, yellow, white, black: the generating cycle of the five elements. Wood gives rise to Fire, Fire to Earth, Earth to Metal, Metal to Water."},{"tag":"✨ A constellation","q":"What are those little connected dots above the yellow tiger's head?","a":"A constellation; some say it's the Big Dipper. The sky, the stars and the five directions, all packed into one frame: The Five Tigers is like a miniature map of the universe."},{"tag":"🗡️ Lords of Five Camps","q":"Why do the tigers come with a sword and a command flag?","a":"Because this is a heavenly army. In Mother Goddess temples, the five Lord Tigers are called quan Ngũ dinh, the officers in charge of five army camps. The sword means authority; the flag means command."},{"tag":"📜 Command tablet","q":"What's the little tablet under the yellow tiger's paws?","a":"A command tablet, a sign of divine authority. The writing on it differs from copy to copy; some show the character Vương, 'king'."},{"tag":"🏮 The lowest altar","q":"Where is this painting worshipped?","a":"In temples and shrines to the Mother Goddess, the five tigers sit at the lowest altar, just below the main one, guarding the temple door. Offerings to the Lord Tigers are usually raw meat and raw eggs. At Tết, the Lunar New Year, many families hang a tiger painting to keep their homes safe.","s":"At the lowest altar in Mother Goddess temples, guarding the door; offerings are usually raw meat and raw eggs."},{"tag":"🖌️ Half print, half paint","q":"Look really close at the tiger's fur. What do you see?","a":"Layers of color blended by hand, shading from dark to light little by little. That's the big difference between Hàng Trống and Đông Hồ: Đông Hồ prints flat colors from woodblocks, while Hàng Trống prints only the outlines and paints the colors in by hand."}]},"to-ngoc-van-thieu-nu-ben-hoa-hue":{"title":"Young Woman with Lilies","artist":"Tô Ngọc Vân (1906–1954)","date":"1943","medium":"Oil","where":"Whereabouts of the original unknown (the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts was still searching for it as of December 2025)","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts (home of its “twin”, Young Woman with Lotus Flowers)","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Indochinese art","oil painting","áo dài","Tô Ngọc Vân"],"hook":"This may well be the most famous Vietnamese painting of all. But here's something few people know: many of the prints out there actually show copies, and the museum is still searching for the original.","intro":"Young Woman with Lilies, an oil painting by Tô Ngọc Vân, {1943|nineteen forty-three}.","openers":["Do you know who the girl in white beside this vase of flowers is?","There's a twist hidden in the painting's Vietnamese name: the flower it names, huệ, isn't the one most Vietnamese would picture.","Try counting how many different shades of white are in this painting."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at one girl beside a vase of lilies…","end":"A quiet moment, a few Easter lilies and so many shades of white. Now you understand why people can look at Young Woman with Lilies for a lifetime and never tire of it.","img":[{"by":"Tô Ngọc Vân · image circulated on Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👁️ A dreamy gaze","q":"Have you noticed the young woman's eyes?","a":"She isn't looking at us. Her eyes are half-closed, her head tilted toward the vase, as if lost in some very private thought. It's that quiet moment that makes it so hard to look away."},{"tag":"🌸 Huệ tây = Easter lily","q":"So what flower is it, really?","a":"The Vietnamese title says huệ, but this is huệ tây, also called loa kèn: the Easter lily, which blooms pure white in Hanoi around late March and early April. It's nothing like the native huệ, the tuberose people offer with incense. The lily stands for purity.","s":"Huệ tây, the Easter lily of April in Hanoi, not the tuberose offered with incense. It stands for purity."},{"tag":"✋ A closed curve","q":"One hand is raised to her hair. Why?","a":"That gesture closes the composition: her arm, her head and the flowers form a curve that wraps around her face. Your eye travels around it, then comes to rest on her face."},{"tag":"🤍 A gentle touch","q":"And the other hand?","a":"It barely touches a petal. Not holding, not picking. A gesture so light, as if she's afraid of hurting the flower."},{"tag":"🤍 White on white","q":"How many shades of white can you count?","a":"A white áo dài, the traditional long dress, white flowers, a pale vase, yet no two whites are the same. The painter mixed white with creamy yellow, blue and pink into countless shades. Painting white on white and keeping it all distinct: that's the masterful challenge of this work."},{"tag":"🏺 A touch of blue","q":"What's the blue-and-white vase doing here?","a":"In a picture that's all yellow and white, the blue patterns on the vase are a rare accent that pulls your eye toward the flowers."},{"tag":"🧑‍🎨 A model named Sáu","q":"Who was the young woman in the painting?","a":"According to the painter's son, she was a professional model named Sáu, whom Tô Ngọc Vân had hired since {1939|nineteen thirty-nine}. She later sat for Nguyễn Gia Trí and Trần Văn Cẩn as well. By the time the son went looking for her, she had passed away.","s":"According to the painter's son, it was Sáu, a professional model he had hired since {1939|nineteen thirty-nine}."},{"tag":"🔎 Where's the original?","q":"Why isn't one of Vietnam's most famous paintings a National Treasure?","a":"According to one expert, because no one has been able to verify which one is the original. The painting passed through many collectors, the museum once hung a copy, and a book from {1983|nineteen eighty-three} even printed a fake by mistake. In {December 2025|December twenty twenty-five}, the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts said it was still searching for the original.","s":"According to one expert, the original has never been verified: it changed hands many times, and copies and fakes exist. The museum is still searching."},{"tag":"👯 Its twin painting","q":"This painting has a twin sister?","a":"Yes: Young Woman with Lotus Flowers, with the same model, held by the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts. After restoration, it went on public display for the first time in {December 2025|December twenty twenty-five}."}]},"to-ngoc-van-hai-thieu-nu-va-em-be":{"title":"Two Young Women and a Child","artist":"Tô Ngọc Vân (1906–1954)","date":"1944","medium":"Oil on canvas","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Indochinese art","oil painting","national treasure","Tô Ngọc Vân"],"hook":"Two young women chatting on a porch, a little boy playing on the floor. A perfectly ordinary scene. But that boy in red is the painter's own son.","intro":"Two Young Women and a Child, oil on canvas, by Tô Ngọc Vân, {1944|nineteen forty-four}. A National Treasure, now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Did you know that, according to the painter's family, this painting once had a name tied to a flower?","One color repeats several times in this painting, and it's probably no accident.","Try to work out what shape the three figures in this painting make."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at one quiet afternoon on a porch…","end":"A peaceful family afternoon, preserved in warm yellow and a flower that blooms at dawn and fades by nightfall. Now you understand why people can look at it for hours.","img":[{"by":"Tô Ngọc Vân · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👦 The painter's son","q":"Who's the little boy in red in the corner?","a":"According to Tô Ngọc Thành himself, the painter's son, it's him at the age of four. The picture was painted right in the family's home on Khâm Thiên Street."},{"tag":"👩 Mai the teacher","q":"And the young woman in yellow sitting on the bamboo daybed?","a":"Also according to Thành, the model was Mai, a teacher and a friend of the painter's wife. The girl in white sitting on the floor was the painter's niece."},{"tag":"🔺 Triangular composition","q":"Can you see what shape the three figures form?","a":"A triangle: the woman in yellow at the top, the girl in white and the little boy at the two bottom corners. The triangular composition of classical Western painting brings calm and balance to a thoroughly Vietnamese scene."},{"tag":"🌺 The cotton rose","q":"What flower is that in the background?","a":"According to the painter's family, it's phù dung, the cotton rose, a flower that blooms in the morning and fades by night. Tô Ngọc Vân compared a young woman's life to this flower, and the painting's first title was Young Women and Cotton Roses.","s":"According to the painter's family, the cotton rose, which blooms at dawn and fades by night; the painting's first title was Young Women and Cotton Roses."},{"tag":"🌸 Three touches of pink","q":"Have you noticed how many times pink shows up?","a":"The flower in the hand of the woman in yellow, the hair clip of the girl in white, and the flowers behind them. Three touches of pink, as if echoing the flower in the painting's first title. That's one reading; you can check it for yourself."},{"tag":"🤍 The girl in white","q":"What is the girl in white doing?","a":"Sitting on the floor, looking up and listening. Her white áo dài, the traditional long dress, fans out over the tiles, against the warm yellow of the woman seated above."},{"tag":"☀️ Warm yellow tones","q":"Why does the whole painting seem bathed in sunlight?","a":"Warm yellow covers everything: the clothes, the bamboo daybed, the floor, the wall. According to its National Treasure dossier, that yellow binds people and nature into one harmonious whole."},{"tag":"🎋 A Hanoi porch","q":"What does the bamboo blind overhead tell us?","a":"A bamboo blind, a bamboo daybed, a porch: everyday Vietnamese things placed in a composition learned from classical Western painting."},{"tag":"💰 200 đồng (1965)","q":"What price was this National Treasure once sold for?","a":"Around {1965|nineteen sixty-five}, its keeper, the physician Nguyễn Tấn Gi Trọng, a friend of the painter, let the museum have it for {200 đồng|two hundred dong}. And in {1970|nineteen seventy}, Tô Ngọc Thành was surprised to find prints of it being sold as souvenirs in Prague.","s":"Around {1965|nineteen sixty-five}, it went to the museum for {200 đồng|two hundred dong}; in {1970|nineteen seventy}, prints of it were even sold as souvenirs in Prague."}]},"nguyen-phan-chanh-choi-o-an-quan":{"title":"Playing Ô Ăn Quan","artist":"Nguyễn Phan Chánh (1892–1984)","date":"1931","medium":"Silk","where":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum, Japan","place":{"name":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum","prov":"Abroad · Japan"},"tags":["silk painting","Indochinese art","folk games","Nguyễn Phan Chánh"],"hook":"Four little girls sit right on the ground, playing a familiar pebble game. But look closely: almost every eye in the picture is fixed on a single hand.","intro":"Playing Ô Ăn Quan, a silk painting by Nguyễn Phan Chánh, {1931|nineteen thirty-one}.","openers":["Can you guess which country this silk painting is kept in today?","One thing about this painting was once dismissed by a French official as not to buyers' taste.","Try to find the game board in this painting. Hint: don't look on a table."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single painting on silk…","end":"A children's game, a humble country brown, and one hand at the center of it all. Now you understand why Playing Ô Ăn Quan is seen as a milestone of Vietnamese silk painting.","img":[{"by":"","lic":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"✋ The focal point","q":"Did you notice the hand making a move?","a":"Every gaze points to it. The painter said he deliberately had the youngest girl make the first move, so the whole group would focus on a single spot. That hand is the heart of the whole painting."},{"tag":"⚖️ Three against one","q":"Why are the girls sitting three against one?","a":"The painter said he put one girl on one side and three huddled together on the other, so the composition wouldn't feel scattered and the light and dark areas would balance out."},{"tag":"🪨 A board on the ground","q":"So where's the game board?","a":"Drawn right on the ground: small squares hold pebbles, the game pieces, and the two end squares are the 'mandarin' squares. In the old days, ô ăn quan was a game for girls of about seven to ten."},{"tag":"🧣 A winter's day","q":"What does the girl's headscarf tell us about the season?","a":"It's a khăn mỏ quạ, the 'crow's beak' headscarf of northern Vietnamese women. The painter's diary describes the children bundled up on cold days in Kim Liên village. This is a winter's day."},{"tag":"🟤 Deep brown tones","q":"Why is the whole painting steeped in deep brown?","a":"It's the brown of village clothing, with touches of black, yellow and red. In {1932|nineteen thirty-two}, when the painting was sold in Paris, a French official even complained the colors were too dull for French buyers' taste. Yet that subdued palette later became the signature of Nguyễn Phan Chánh's silk paintings."},{"tag":"💧 Paint, then wash","q":"How did he make the silk look as soft as mist?","a":"Nguyễn Phan Chánh would paint a layer of color, let it dry, wash the painting in water, then paint and wash again, many times over. The color soaks so deep into the silk that both sides look almost the same."},{"tag":"✍️ Poem and seal","q":"What are those lines of Chinese characters in the corner?","a":"His silk paintings often carry a poem in Chinese characters, a red seal and a signature. The poem doesn't necessarily describe the scene in the picture."},{"tag":"🗾 Fukuoka, Japan","q":"Where is this painting today?","a":"In Japan, at the Fukuoka Asian Art Museum. In {1932|nineteen thirty-two}, it sold in Paris for two thousand francs and was printed in color in a famous French magazine. The painter also made several other versions of the same subject."}]},"tran-van-can-em-thuy":{"title":"Little Thúy","artist":"Trần Văn Cẩn (1910–1994)","date":"1943","medium":"Oil on canvas","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["portrait","oil painting","national treasure","Trần Văn Cẩn"],"hook":"An eight-year-old girl sits in a rattan chair, her hands folded together. Some call this painting the Mona Lisa of Vietnam. Why?","intro":"Little Thúy, oil on canvas, by Trần Văn Cẩn, {1943|nineteen forty-three}. A National Treasure, at the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Did you know the girl in this painting was a real person, and lived until {2024|twenty twenty-four}?","One part of this painting was once the most badly damaged, and you're probably looking straight at it.","Take a close look at the girl's dress: is it really white?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a portrait of one little girl…","end":"A little girl, a pink silk dress, and a gaze that reaches across more than eighty years. Now you understand why people call Little Thúy the Mona Lisa of Vietnam.","img":[{"by":"","lic":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"👁️ The gaze","q":"Who are those shining black eyes looking at?","a":"Straight out at us, round and shining black. Restorer Caroline Fry called the painting the Mona Lisa of Vietnam, and British composer Paul Zetter felt the girl seemed to follow the viewer with her eyes."},{"tag":"👧 Nguyễn Minh Thúy","q":"Who was Little Thúy?","a":"Her name was Nguyễn Minh Thúy, born in {1935|nineteen thirty-five}, and she called the painter 'uncle'. At eight, she sat for him through many sessions on Hàng Cót Street. She grew up to be a teacher and passed away in {2024|twenty twenty-four}, at the age of eighty-nine."},{"tag":"🎀 Pale pink silk","q":"The dress looks white, but what color is it really?","a":"It's pale pink Hà Đông silk. It was seeing the girl in that very dress that made the painter want to paint her."},{"tag":"🤲 A little shy","q":"What do those folded hands tell us?","a":"A touch of shyness, from an eight-year-old made to sit still in front of an easel. Her body leans slightly forward, her hands resting obediently in her lap."},{"tag":"🪑 The rattan chair","q":"What role does the rattan chair play?","a":"The brown of the rattan wraps around the pale dress and makes her face stand out. Broad areas of color, loose brushwork: restorer Caroline Fry praised that very style for its Eastern spirit."},{"tag":"🩹 Saving the painting","q":"Which part of the painting was once the most badly damaged?","a":"The face itself. By {2003|two thousand three}, the painting was covered in dust, soot and insect droppings, and the paint was blistering and flaking. Caroline Fry, an expert from Australia, worked for months at the museum and handed the painting back on {June 28, 2004|June twenty-eighth, two thousand four}."},{"tag":"🔎 Lost and found","q":"How did the painting go missing?","a":"In {1943|nineteen forty-three}, it won first prize at an exhibition in Hanoi, then went missing during the war. One source says the museum bought it back in {1964|nineteen sixty-four} for {300 đồng|three hundred dong}; another says it was found in a barber's house."},{"tag":"🎻 From painting to music","q":"Can a painting give birth to a piece of music?","a":"Yes. After seeing the painting, composer Paul Zetter wrote a minuet dedicated to Little Thúy, and in {2024|twenty twenty-four}, he gave the rights to the piece to the museum."}]},"trong-dong-ngoc-lu":{"title":"The Ngọc Lũ Bronze Drum","artist":"People of the Đông Sơn culture","date":"About 2,000–2,500 years ago (3rd–2nd century BCE)","medium":"Bronze","where":"National Museum of Vietnamese History, 1 Tràng Tiền, Hanoi","place":{"name":"National Museum of Vietnamese History","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Đông Sơn","bronze drum","national treasure","archaeology"],"hook":"A drum dug up by chance while villagers were building a dike, now at the top of Vietnam's very first list of National Treasures. Its face holds the whole world of the ancient Vietnamese, if you're willing to look really slowly.","intro":"The Ngọc Lũ bronze drum, from the Đông Sơn culture, some two thousand to two and a half thousand years old. The drawing you're seeing recreates the patterns on the face of a Đông Sơn drum.","openers":["Why do the people, deer and birds on this drum all move counterclockwise?","There's a detail right in the center of this drum: a number that still has no agreed explanation.","Try to find a row of drums on this drum's face, with someone pounding down on them with a long pole."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single bronze drum…","end":"One drum that holds the sun, the harvests, the festivals and the boats of the ancient Vietnamese. Now you understand why the Ngọc Lũ drum is a National Treasure.","img":[{"by":"Drawing of Đông Sơn drum patterns: Tom Mai","lic":"Public domain"},{"by":"Photo: VuThiAnh · National Museum of Vietnamese History","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🥁 Dug up building a dike","q":"What does the real drum look like?","a":"Sixty-three centimeters tall, with a face nearly eighty centimeters across, covered in a gray-green patina. Around {1893|eighteen ninety-three}, villagers building a dike dug it up and took it to be worshipped in the Ngọc Lũ village communal house. In {1903|nineteen oh three}, it was brought to Hanoi.","s":"Around {1893|eighteen ninety-three}, villagers building a dike dug it up and enshrined it in the Ngọc Lũ communal house."},{"tag":"☀️ A 14-ray sun","q":"How many points does the star in the center of the drum have?","a":"On the Ngọc Lũ drum, fourteen. Most researchers see it as the sun, tied to the sun worship of rice-growing peoples. Why fourteen? No one has been able to explain that to this day.","s":"Fourteen points: the sun of a rice-growing people. Why fourteen? No one has been able to explain it yet."},{"tag":"🦚 Peacock feathers","q":"What's tucked between the points of the star?","a":"Triangular motifs that look like peacock feathers. From the center, concentric bands of patterns ripple outward; the Ngọc Lũ drum has sixteen bands in all."},{"tag":"🪶 People dressed as birds","q":"What are those people in feathered headdresses doing?","a":"They're dressed up as birds; some carry spears, some play the khèn, a bamboo mouth organ. Archaeologists agree this is a ritual with music. Some read it as a festival praying for a good harvest; others think it's a funeral rite.","s":"A ritual with music: people dressed as birds, holding spears, playing mouth organs. Harvest festival or funeral? The debate goes on."},{"tag":"🥁 Drums on a drum","q":"Can you see the drums pictured right on the face of the drum?","a":"A row of drums, with someone pounding down on them with a long pole. That's direct evidence: bronze drums were instruments for community rituals."},{"tag":"🛖 Boat-roofed stilt house","q":"What does this house with a boat-shaped roof tell us?","a":"It's a stilt house with a curved, boat-shaped roof and bird heads on the gables. Some believe it was a ritual house of the Lạc Việt, the ancient Vietnamese, and a distant ancestor of the village communal house roof."},{"tag":"🌾 Pounding rice","q":"What's this pair holding pestles doing?","a":"Pounding rice. The pestles even have feathers attached, so many think this may be part of a harvest ritual of wet-rice farmers."},{"tag":"🦌 Herds of deer","q":"What animals are on the outer bands?","a":"Herds of deer, along with flocks of flying birds, all heading in the same direction."},{"tag":"🐦 The Lạc bird?","q":"What are the long-beaked birds on the outermost band?","a":"People usually call them Lạc birds, but that's just a conventional name. Some say they're egrets, herons or cranes; others say cormorants. The Ngọc Lũ drum has thirty-six of them: eighteen in flight and eighteen standing.","s":"They're usually called Lạc birds, but the real species is still debated. The Ngọc Lũ drum has thirty-six of them."},{"tag":"↺ Counterclockwise","q":"Have you noticed that everything moves in the same direction?","a":"People, deer and birds all circle the star counterclockwise. Why? There's still no agreed answer, only theories, such as a link with the way the sun's shadow moves."},{"tag":"🚣 Six boats","q":"What else is on the side of the drum?","a":"Six boats, carrying people in feathered headdresses, spearmen and a helmsman. Are they warships, racing boats, or boats carrying the souls of the dead? Researchers are still debating."}]},"quan-am-but-thap":{"title":"The Thousand-Eyed, Thousand-Armed Quan Âm of Bút Tháp Pagoda","artist":"An artisan surnamed Trương","date":"1656 (autumn of the Bính Thân year)","medium":"Wood, red lacquer and gilding","where":"Bút Tháp Pagoda (Ninh Phúc Temple), Bắc Ninh","place":{"name":"Bút Tháp Pagoda","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Buddhist sculpture","Lê dynasty","national treasure","Quan Âm"],"hook":"How many arms does this statue have? Ask ten people, and you might get ten different numbers.","intro":"The Thousand-Eyed, Thousand-Armed Quan Âm, Bodhisattva of Compassion, at Bút Tháp Pagoda in Bắc Ninh: red-lacquered, gilded wood, carved in {1656|sixteen fifty-six}.","openers":["Why is there an eye in the palm of every small hand on this statue?","This statue has a rare detail: it tells us the year it was carved, and even who carved it.","Stop and look at the hands joined before the chest: the two palms don't actually touch."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours standing before a single statue…","end":"Nearly a thousand hands, each with its own eye: a lesson in compassion that knows how to see and how to act. Now you understand why people can stand before this statue for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Benjamin Smith (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Khangoii (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Khangoii (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"📏 3.7 meters","q":"How tall is the statue?","a":"Nearly four meters, including the pedestal. It was carved in {1656|sixteen fifty-six}, during the Restored Lê period, and was named a National Treasure in the very first round, in {2012|twenty twelve}."},{"tag":"🖐️ Nearly 1,000 hands","q":"So how many arms are there?","a":"Most sources count forty-two large arms, plus a halo of nearly a thousand small hands arranged in layers. Every book counts differently: {952|nine hundred fifty-two}, {958|nine hundred fifty-eight}… Because 'a thousand' here isn't a count; it's a symbol of perfect completeness.","s":"About forty-two large arms and nearly a thousand small hands; every book counts differently, because 'a thousand' here is a symbol of completeness."},{"tag":"👁️ An eye in each palm","q":"Did you notice there's an eye in the palm of each small hand?","a":"The eye is knowing; the hand is doing. Only by knowing can you act rightly, and only by acting do you come to know deeply. A thousand eyes, a thousand hands: compassion that sees everything and acts to help all living beings."},{"tag":"✖️ 40 × 25 = 1,000","q":"How do forty-two large arms add up to a thousand?","a":"By the convention for thousand-armed statues: apart from the two joined hands, each of the remaining forty arms saves beings in twenty-five realms. Forty times twenty-five: exactly one thousand."},{"tag":"🙂 Eleven faces","q":"Why does the statue have as many as eleven faces?","a":"The faces are stacked in tiers, each with its own expression, symbolizing how Quan Âm appears to help in every situation. A Tibetan legend says that when the vow to save all beings wavered, Quan Âm's body shattered, and Amitabha Buddha pieced it back together with eleven faces and a thousand arms.","s":"Each face is different: Quan Âm appears in every situation. A Tibetan legend says Quan Âm's body once shattered, and Amitabha Buddha rebuilt it with eleven faces."},{"tag":"🧘 Amitabha","q":"Who's sitting at the very top?","a":"A small statue of Amitabha Buddha, placed at the top in reverence to the Buddha."},{"tag":"🙏 Hollow joined palms","q":"Why don't the hands joined before the chest press tightly together?","a":"The palms don't touch, leaving an empty space between them. One researcher calls this gesture hư tâm hiệp chưởng, 'joined palms with a hollow center', and links it to Tibetan statues, where the palms sometimes hold a jewel symbolizing the mind set on enlightenment."},{"tag":"✨ Red lacquer and gold","q":"What expression does the main face wear?","a":"Gentle, with eyes cast down. Its coat of red lacquer and gold leaf, done with traditional Vietnamese lacquer techniques, makes the face seem to glow in the darkness of the main hall."},{"tag":"🐉 Dragon under the lotus","q":"Who's the creature beneath the lotus throne?","a":"According to some sources, it's a Dragon King holding up the lotus throne, a guardian of the Buddha's teachings. The lotus throne stands for purity rising out of the mud."},{"tag":"✍️ Inscribed in 1656","q":"Where is this statue signed?","a":"An inscription on the statue says it was made in the autumn of the Bính Thân year, that is, {1656|sixteen fifty-six}, along with the carver's name; later studies attribute it to an artisan surnamed Trương. An ancient statue that records both when and by whom it was carved is a real rarity."}]},"chua-mot-cot":{"title":"The One Pillar Pagoda","artist":"Built under Emperor Lý Thái Tông; the current structure was rebuilt under architect Nguyễn Bá Lăng (1955)","date":"1049 (rebuilt in 1955)","medium":"Wood, stone, tile","where":"Chùa Một Cột Street, Ba Đình, Hanoi","place":{"name":"One Pillar Pagoda","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Lý dynasty","pagoda","Hanoi","Buddhist architecture"],"hook":"A pagoda standing on just one pillar. Its story begins with a dream, and once, it was blown apart in a single night.","intro":"The One Pillar Pagoda in Hanoi, formally named Diên Hựu. The first pagoda on this site was built in {1049|ten forty-nine}, under Emperor Lý Thái Tông.","openers":["Did you know the One Pillar Pagoda has a formal name that means 'lasting blessings'?","Here's a detail from this pagoda's history: a huge bell once lay out in a rice field, where turtles made their home.","Try to spot the pair of dragons facing the moon on the roof, and the four dragon heads at its four corners."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours gazing at one tiny pagoda…","end":"A dream, a pillar, a lotus in a pond. Now you understand why this tiny pagoda has stood in the hearts of Hanoians for nearly a thousand years.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0)","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Firmin-André Salles, 1896","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🌙 A dream in 1049","q":"Why would a pagoda stand on a single pillar?","a":"According to the Complete Annals of Đại Việt, an old royal chronicle, in {1049|ten forty-nine} Emperor Lý Thái Tông dreamed that Quan Âm, seated on a lotus throne, led him up onto a platform. His courtiers saw it as a bad omen. The monk Thiền Tuệ advised him to raise a stone pillar from the ground and set Quan Âm's lotus throne on top, just as in the dream.","s":"Emperor Lý Thái Tông dreamed of Quan Âm on a lotus throne; the monk Thiền Tuệ advised raising a stone pillar with the lotus throne on top, as in the dream."},{"tag":"🪷 Diên Hựu","q":"What's the pagoda's real name?","a":"Diên Hựu, meaning 'lasting blessings': when it was finished, monks walked around it chanting sutras for the emperor's long life. The square shrine on the pillar is called Liên Hoa Đài, the Lotus Platform."},{"tag":"🏛️ One pillar","q":"How big is this pillar?","a":"About {1.2 meters|one point two meters} across and roughly four meters tall from the ground. The pillar is the stem of a lotus rising out of the pond."},{"tag":"🌿 Like a flower's sepals","q":"What are the slanted wooden braces under the shrine floor for?","a":"They hold up the shrine, fanning out like the sepals that cradle a lotus flower."},{"tag":"🐉 Dragons facing the moon","q":"What's on the roof?","a":"Four sweeping, upturned corners shaped like dragon heads, and on the ridge, a pair of dragons facing the moon."},{"tag":"◻️ Linh Chiểu Pond","q":"Why is the pond square?","a":"Linh Chiểu Pond is square, about sixteen meters on each side. One reading says the square pond stands for the earth; that's one interpretation. The more familiar meaning: a pure lotus rising out of the mud."},{"tag":"🗺️ A Lý-era complex","q":"Was the pagoda this small back in the Lý dynasty?","a":"No. In {1105|eleven oh five}, Emperor Lý Nhân Tông had it expanded: digging Linh Chiểu Pond and adding Bích Trì Pond, galleries, bridges and a stupa. One researcher sees the whole complex as a mandala, a model of the Buddhist universe."},{"tag":"📷 In 1896","q":"What's special about this photo from {1896|eighteen ninety-six}?","a":"This is the One Pillar Pagoda in a photo taken in {1896|eighteen ninety-six}. The pagoda we see today is not that same building."},{"tag":"💥 1954","q":"What happened one night in {September 1954|September nineteen fifty-four}?","a":"Just before French troops withdrew from Hanoi, explosives were planted at the pagoda, bringing down the shrine. In {1955|nineteen fifty-five}, architect Nguyễn Bá Lăng led its reconstruction, following the Nguyễn-dynasty design. The pagoda you see today is that rebuilt version."},{"tag":"🐢 The Quy Điền Bell","q":"Why did the pagoda's bell end up in a rice field, home to turtles?","a":"In {1080|ten eighty}, under the Lý dynasty, the pagoda cast the Giác Thế bell. Too heavy to hang, it was left out in a rice field, where turtles moved in: hence the name Quy Điền, 'turtle field'. In {1426|fourteen twenty-six}, Ming troops from China broke it up for its bronze to make weapons.","card":{"big":"🔔","sub":"The Quy Điền “Turtle Field” Bell"}}]},"tien-quan-ca":{"title":"Army March","artist":"Văn Cao (1923–1995)","date":"Late 1944","medium":"March","where":"Written in an attic room at 45 Nguyễn Thượng Hiền Street, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Nguyễn Thượng Hiền Street (the attic where it was written)","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["national anthem","march","August Revolution","Văn Cao"],"hook":"The song an entire nation stands at attention for at every flag ceremony was written in a cramped attic, during the famine of {1944|nineteen forty-four}, by a twenty-one-year-old who had never held a gun.","intro":"Army March, Vietnam's national anthem, by composer Văn Cao. This video doesn't quote the lyrics; it just tells the story behind the song.","openers":["Did you know Vietnam's national anthem was never written to be a national anthem?","Here's a detail from the pottery village of Bát Tràng: the very first printing of this song was corrected with lime juice.","Next time you hear the anthem, listen to the very first note: it was once shortened to make the song punchier."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why millions of people can be moved to tears singing one song together…","end":"A march as short as a bugle call, born amid hunger and cold, written backwards on a slab of stone so it could be printed. Now you understand why, every time it rings out, it still brings people to tears.","img":[{"by":"Photo: rusticus80 (CC BY-SA 2.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"by":"Photo: P. Hughes (CC BY 4.0)","lic":"CC BY 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Liftold (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Greenknight dv (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🕯️ Amid a famine","q":"How did this song come into the world?","a":"In late {1944|nineteen forty-four}, famine was spreading across northern Vietnam. Văn Cao was twenty-one and penniless; some nights he burned his own manuscripts for warmth. He recalled walking through streets where people were dying of hunger, and that night he wrote the first notes.","s":"Late {1944|nineteen forty-four}, in the middle of a famine: a twenty-one-year-old burned his manuscripts for warmth and wrote the first notes.","card":{"big":"1944","sub":"The attic at 45 Nguyễn Thượng Hiền, Hanoi"}},{"tag":"🎺 Like a bugle call","q":"Was this song written to be a national anthem?","a":"No. It was the first task a cadre of the Việt Minh, the independence movement, gave him: write a song for a military training class. He had never been to the resistance zone, so he wrote something soldiers could sing right away, short and sharp like a bugle call.","card":{"big":"🎺","sub":"A song for a military training class"}},{"tag":"🍋 Backwards on stone","q":"Where was the first printing made?","a":"In the pottery village of Bát Tràng, in {November 1944|November nineteen forty-four}, in the underground newspaper Độc Lập, 'Independence'. Văn Cao himself wrote the song backwards onto stone for lithographic printing, and used lime juice instead of acid to correct the plate. That stone was hidden in the village pond and only pulled out in {1984|nineteen eighty-four}.","s":"In the pottery village of Bát Tràng: Văn Cao wrote the song backwards on a lithography stone, using lime juice instead of acid."},{"tag":"🏯 Tân Trào, August 1945","q":"When did it become the national anthem?","a":"In mid-{August 1945|August nineteen forty-five}, at Tân Trào, President Hồ Chí Minh chose the Army March because it was short, strong and easy to sing. In {1946|nineteen forty-six}, the first National Assembly officially adopted it, and the first Constitution wrote it in."},{"tag":"🌳 The Tân Trào banyan","q":"Where did soldiers first sing this song?","a":"According to the composer's family, the Liberation Army unit sang the Army March at its send-off ceremony beneath the banyan tree of Tân Trào."},{"tag":"🏛️ Opera House, 8/17/1945","q":"Why is the Hanoi Opera House tied to this song?","a":"On {August 17, 1945|August seventeenth, nineteen forty-five}, a rally at the Opera House suddenly changed course: a red flag with a yellow star unfurled from the balcony, and thousands of people sang together. Văn Cao, though ill, still made it there, and he broke down in tears."},{"tag":"🎼 Two notes","q":"Did you know the opening note was once changed?","a":"Before {September 2, 1945|September second, nineteen forty-five}, conductor Đinh Ngọc Liên and Văn Cao agreed to change two notes, shortening the opening note to make the song more vigorous. The version sung today is that revised one.","card":{"big":"♪","sub":"The opening note was shortened"}},{"tag":"🏁 Nearly 1,500 entries","q":"Why is only the first verse sung?","a":"The Army March has two verses, but since {1955|nineteen fifty-five}, only the first has been used officially. In {1981|nineteen eighty-one}, nearly fifteen hundred songs were entered in a contest for a new anthem, but in the end the National Assembly kept the Army March."},{"tag":"🤝 Donated in 2016","q":"Who does the national anthem belong to?","a":"On {July 15, 2016|July fifteenth, twenty sixteen}, Văn Cao's family officially donated the song to the State. As the composer himself once said, the song had long since stopped being his alone; it belonged to every Vietnamese person.","card":{"big":"2016","sub":"The song belongs to everyone"}}]},"quan-ho-nguoi-oi-nguoi-o-dung-ve":{"title":"Quan họ: “Dear Friend, Please Stay”","artist":"Quan họ folk song (popular version arranged by composer Xuân Tứ in 1958)","date":"A centuries-old tradition; arranged in 1958","medium":"Folk song, call-and-response singing without accompaniment","where":"49 original quan họ villages on both banks of the Cầu River, Bắc Ninh","place":{"name":"Diềm village (Viêm Xá), where Vua Bà, founder of quan họ, is worshipped","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["quan họ","UNESCO","folk song","Bắc Ninh"],"hook":"They sing to each other all night long, call each other 'big brother' and 'big sister', and hold each other dear. But there's a village rule: quan họ partners never marry each other.","intro":"Quan họ folk songs from Bắc Ninh in northern Vietnam, inscribed by UNESCO in {2009|two thousand nine}. This video doesn't quote any lyrics; it just tells the story behind them.","openers":["Did you know this beloved quan họ farewell song was reworked by a composer in {1958|nineteen fifty-eight}?","A detail about the nón quai thao, the wide silk-strapped hat: in the old days, quan họ singers sometimes hid their faces behind it.","Next time you hear quan họ, listen for the nảy: a sound caught for an instant in the throat, then released."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can sit and listen to quan họ all night long…","end":"A night of singing without instruments, a bond never allowed to become a marriage, and a plea for the guests to stay as day breaks. Now you understand why people can listen to quan họ their whole lives.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Chrisvomberg (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Viethavvh (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Vanminhhanoi (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🤝 Quan họ partners","q":"Who are the liền anh and liền chị?","a":"They're the quan họ singers: liền anh are the men, liền chị the women. A group of men from one village bonds with a group of women from another, and only groups bonded this way sing together. They sing in call and response: one side offers a melody, and the other answers with a similar tune but different words."},{"tag":"🏘️ 49 original villages","q":"Why are there forty-nine original quan họ villages?","a":"The forty-nine villages lie along both banks of the Cầu River. Legend tells of Vua Bà, the Lady King, a daughter of the legendary Hùng kings, who taught the people of Diềm village to plant rice, weave silk and sing. One version says she and forty-nine people became sworn brothers and sisters, then spread out to teach quan họ.","card":{"big":"49","sub":"original quan họ villages on both banks of the Cầu River"}},{"tag":"💔 The no-marriage rule","q":"Why aren't quan họ partners allowed to marry?","a":"It's a village custom; some places even wrote it into their village charters, and UNESCO mentions it too, though some couples still broke the rule. One elderly quan họ singer believes it's this very rule that gives quan họ melodies their gentle, lingering sadness.","card":{"big":"💔","sub":"Loving, but never marrying"}},{"tag":"🌙 All-night singing","q":"How long does a quan họ singing night last?","a":"It can run from dusk until dawn, about ten hours, with no microphones and no instruments. A singing night must have all three stages: lề lối, the old formal melodies; giọng vặt, freer songs; and finally giã bạn, the farewell, as dawn approaches.","card":{"big":"7 p.m. → 5 a.m.","sub":"One singing night"}},{"tag":"🎙️ Vang, rền, nền, nảy","q":"What do vang, rền, nền and nảy mean?","a":"Vang is a voice that carries far. Rền is a steady vibration that never runs out of breath. Nảy is a sound caught very quickly in the throat, then released. As for nền, people still argue about it.","card":{"big":"Vang · Rền · Nền · Nảy","sub":"Four words for the quan họ voice"}},{"tag":"👒 The quai thao hat","q":"What's this hat, nearly eighty centimeters wide, for?","a":"It's the nón quai thao, with silk straps. In the old days, duet singers sometimes hid their faces behind it while singing."},{"tag":"👘 The áo mớ ba","q":"What do the liền chị wear?","a":"The áo mớ ba: three layered tunics, a peach-pink bodice, a crow's-beak headscarf, and a sash tied like a flower petal at the front."},{"tag":"🎩 Gauze robe and turban","q":"And the liền anh?","a":"A black gauze robe over a long tunic, a pleated turban, a silk umbrella, and humble manners: they call themselves em, 'the younger one', and their partners 'big brother', 'big sister'. No one ranks above anyone else."},{"tag":"🛶 Festival singing","q":"Is singing on boats the old-style quan họ?","a":"Not really. Singing on boats is mostly a festival-day activity. The traditional way is hát canh: at night, indoors, between quan họ groups who have bonded with each other."},{"tag":"🎶 Xuân Tứ, 1958","q":"Is “Dear Friend, Please Stay” really an old song?","a":"Not quite. Today's popular version was reworked in {1958|nineteen fifty-eight} by composer Xuân Tứ from an old quan họ song. He trimmed it down, keeping only the parting, the last words of advice and the longing, and pushed the refrain higher so the plea to stay sounds even more heartfelt.","card":{"big":"1958","sub":"Composer Xuân Tứ reworks the song"}},{"tag":"🌅 Farewell at dawn","q":"Why is the farewell song so sad?","a":"Because it rises after a long night of singing together, as day breaks: the hosts beg their guests to stay and promise to meet again, knowing that by custom, this bond can never become a marriage."}]}}};
